course: biology
lesson: 110
title: Behind the scenes: the zoo vet and the animal hospital
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A big zoo has its own animal hospital with X-ray machines, scanners, a lab and an operating room, and the zoo vet might treat a frog in the morning and a rhino in the afternoon.
explain: The hardest part is that a patient cannot say where it hurts and may be big or dangerous, so vets rely on keepers' notes, weight, dung samples and training, and use sleep medicine only when an animal cannot be handled safely.
explain: Picture a gorilla putting its arm through a special sleeve for a blood test, calmly, for a treat: months of training with rewards mean no sleep medicine is needed, which is much safer for the gorilla.
explain: Doctor Dolittle asks the animals what is wrong and cartoon animals bounce back from any bump; a real zoo vet cannot talk to her patients, so she watches, weighs, scans and tests, and real injuries take real treatment and time.
fact: Big zoos have hospitals with X-ray machines, scanners, labs and operating rooms, because moving a big or wild animal to an outside hospital is hard.
fact: Vets use darts with sleep medicine for animals that cannot be handled safely, because a big wild animal held down awake could hurt itself or people, and every dose must suit the animal's kind and weight.
fact: Some apes are trained to put an arm into a special sleeve for blood tests while awake; gorillas often get heart disease, like people, so zoos check their hearts with scans.
fact: Big cats can break teeth on bones, and a broken tooth can get infected and hurt, so zoo vets do fillings and root canals like dentists, and some have fitted big cats with metal crowns.
fact: Ultrasound uses sound waves to show a baby inside its mother, as for people, and elephants and big cats have been trained to stay still for scans in return for treats.
fact: A new animal is kept apart in quarantine for weeks before joining the others, in case it carries a disease that has not shown yet; the word comes from Italian for «forty days», how long ships once waited in port.
fact: Zoos have scales of every size, from tiny bird scales to platforms for elephants, because weight shows health and is needed to get medicine doses right, and elephants are trained to step on for a treat.
fact: Keepers hide medicine in favourite foods, like a pill in a grape, because animals spit out bitter pills, and some animals get clever at eating around them.
fact: Some zoos run hospitals for hurt wild animals like hedgehogs, owls and foxes, hurt by cars, cats, nets and garden strimmers; in Britain hedgehogs have become much rarer, so every one saved counts.
fact: Many zoo animals live longer than wild ones thanks to food, medicine and safety from predators, but some, like elephants and orcas, have often lived shorter lives in captivity than in the wild.
fact: Old zoo animals get special care, like soft food and pain medicine for stiff joints.
fact: Vets look at dung under a microscope for worm eggs and germs, and they can measure hormones in dung to tell whether an animal is stressed or pregnant without touching it.
exercise: Why does a big zoo have its own animal hospital? | Moving a big or wild animal to an outside hospital is hard | Zoo animals never get ill, but it looks good | Pet vets refuse all zoo animals by law | So that paying visitors can watch the operations through a window
exercise: When does a zoo vet use a dart with sleep medicine? | When the animal cannot be handled safely awake | Every time any animal needs a check | Only to stop the animals making noise | Only for animals smaller than a cat
exercise: Why must a sleep medicine dose be worked out carefully? | It must suit the animal's kind and weight | All animals, big or small, need exactly the same dose | A bigger dose always works better | Only the colour of the dart matters
exercise: How do some zoos take blood from a gorilla without sleep medicine? | It is trained to put its arm in a sleeve for a reward | The keeper holds it down by force | The gorilla takes the blood itself | They wait until it is fast asleep at night and creep in
exercise: Why do zoos scan gorillas' hearts? | Gorillas often get heart disease, like people | Gorillas have no heartbeat at all | To see what they ate for lunch | To find out how clever they are
exercise: Why must a lion's broken tooth be fixed? | It can get infected and hurt | Lions need all their teeth to roar | A broken tooth grows back crooked | Lions are vain about their smiles
exercise: What can an ultrasound scan show a vet? | A baby growing inside its mother | The animal's dreams while it sleeps | What the animal is thinking about | The true colour of its blood inside
exercise: Why is a new animal kept apart for weeks? | It might carry a disease that has not shown yet | It must learn all of the zoo's rules before it can meet others | The other animals need time to vote | It is too shy to meet visitors
exercise: Where does the word «quarantine» come from? | Italian for «forty days» that ships waited | Latin for «sick animal» | A famous vet called Quarantino | French for «a small room»
exercise: Why do zoos weigh their animals? | Weight shows health and sets medicine doses | To decide how much to charge visitors for each ticket | To see which animal wins a prize | To know how big a cage to sell
exercise: How does a zoo weigh an elephant? | It is trained to step on a giant scale for a treat | Keepers lift it with a big crane on to the scale every day | They guess from the size of its footprint | They weigh its food and dung instead
exercise: Why do keepers hide pills inside grapes? | Animals spit out bitter pills | Grapes make medicine twice as strong | Monkeys can only swallow round things | Pills melt without a grape around them
exercise: What hurts many hedgehogs brought to zoo wildlife hospitals? | Cars, cats, nets and garden strimmers | Too much hot summer sunshine on their backs | Eating too many grapes from the garden | Other hedgehogs fighting them over food
exercise: Why do many zoo animals live longer than wild ones? | They get food, medicine and safety from predators | The clean zoo air makes animals grow old more slowly | They sleep all day in the zoo | Visitors give them healthy snacks
exercise: Which animals have often lived shorter lives in captivity than in the wild? | Elephants and orcas | Meerkats and tortoises | Hedgehogs and owls | Frogs and lizards
exercise: What can a vet learn from a dung sample? | Worm eggs, germs, and even stress or pregnancy | Only what colour the food was | Exactly how old the animal will live to be before it dies | The animal's favourite keeper
test: In Doctor Dolittle the vet asks animals where it hurts. How does a real zoo vet find out? | She watches, weighs, scans and tests | She asks the animal politely where it hurts | She guesses just from the look on its face | She waits until it gets better alone
test: A new monkey arrives and must stay in quarantine for 40 days. About how many weeks is that? | Nearly 6 weeks | Exactly 4 weeks | About 10 whole weeks | About 2 weeks or so
test: 3 monkeys each need 2 pills a day, one pill in each grape. How many grapes a day? | 6 | 5 | 3 | 9
test: In cartoons an animal loses a tooth and is fine in the next scene. What would happen to a real lion? | The broken tooth could get infected and need a vet | The tooth grows back the next day | Lions do not feel tooth pain | It would just eat with its other teeth
test: «just bung the gorilla to sleep every time, simpler» Why do vets prefer training? | Training with rewards avoids sleep medicine, which is safer | Sleep medicine never works on gorillas because they are too strong | Gorillas enjoy needles | Training is cheaper than food
test: «found a hedgehog in the road, it's hurt» What is the best thing to do? | Ask an adult to call a wildlife hospital or rescue | Leave it in the road so it can walk off when it feels better | Give it a bowl of milk and go | Keep it as a pet in your room
test: What would happen if a new animal with a hidden disease joined the group at once? | The disease could spread to the other animals | The others would make it better | Nothing, as zoo animals cannot catch any diseases | The disease would disappear in the crowd
test: «zoo animals always live longer, the end» What is right? | Many do, but elephants and orcas have often lived shorter lives in captivity | Yes, every zoo animal lives longer | No, all zoo animals die young | No, zoo and wild animals live exactly as long
test: An elephant weighs 4000 kilograms and a tortoise 200. How many tortoises weigh as much as the elephant? | 20 | 200 | 2 | 40
test: «why's the vet looking at poo through a microscope, gross» What is the reason? | She is looking for worm eggs and germs, and can check hormones | She is checking what colour it is | She is studying how to make the best compost for the zoo garden | She lost a contact lens in it
test: A monkey keeps eating the grape and spitting out the pill. What might keepers try? | Hiding it in a different favourite food | Forcing the pill in by hand every day | Stopping its medicine for good | Giving it a bigger grape with two pills
test: 2 lions and 1 elephant are weighed today. How many tails go on the scales? | 3 | 2 | 6 | 12
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of quarantine for an animal newly received at a zoological garden? | The prevention of the transmission of any disease which has not yet become apparent | The instruction of the newly arrived animal in the rules and routines of the establishment | The accustoming of the animal to members of the public | The fattening of the animal prior to display
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the medication of an animal is concealed within a favoured food. | Animals commonly reject bitter tablets offered alone | The food increases the potency of the medicine twofold | Animals are unable to swallow objects of any other form | The medicine dissolves without such a covering
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the longevity of animals kept in zoological gardens. | Many live longer than in the wild, although elephants and orcas have often lived shorter lives in captivity | All live longer in captivity without exception | All live considerably shorter lives in captivity | The longevity of every species is identical in captivity and in the wild
exam: State the information which a veterinary surgeon may obtain from the microscopic and chemical examination of dung. | The presence of worm eggs and germs, and the levels of hormones indicating stress or pregnancy | The precise age at which the animal will die | The identity of the keeper preferred by the animal | Nothing of medical value whatsoever
